1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,370
Throw a coin into the
Trevi Fountain in Rome,

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:07,010
and you're throwing it into water that was
first brought to the city more than two

3
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:10,390
thousand years ago, by an
aqueduct built in nineteen BC.

4
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:11,790
And it's still running.

5
00:00:12,731 --> 00:00:15,501
Ancient Rome was a city of
around a million people.

6
00:00:15,731 --> 00:00:18,361
No pumps, no electricity, no steel pipes.

7
00:00:18,711 --> 00:00:21,051
And yet, fresh water poured
into it day and night,

8
00:00:21,091 --> 00:00:23,101
from springs dozens of miles away.

9
00:00:24,024 --> 00:00:25,594
So how did the Romans do it?

10
00:00:25,644 --> 00:00:29,574
How do you move a river across mountains
and valleys using nothing but gravity?

11
00:00:29,884 --> 00:00:30,954
And why did it all stop?

12
00:00:36,667 --> 00:00:38,357
Rome sits on the river Tiber.

13
00:00:38,467 --> 00:00:40,877
But for drinking, the
Tiber was a bad idea.

14
00:00:41,147 --> 00:00:42,887
The city's sewers emptied into it.

15
00:00:42,927 --> 00:00:43,917
And it often flooded.

16
00:00:44,744 --> 00:00:47,104
For centuries, Romans
drank from the river,

17
00:00:47,144 --> 00:00:49,014
from wells and from local springs.

18
00:00:49,444 --> 00:00:52,314
But as Rome grew into the biggest
city in the Mediterranean,

19
00:00:52,684 --> 00:00:53,574
that wasn't enough.

20
00:00:54,466 --> 00:00:59,346
In three hundred and twelve BC, a magistrate
named Appius Claudius built the first

21
00:00:59,386 --> 00:01:01,196
aqueduct, the Aqua Appia.

22
00:01:01,446 --> 00:01:03,796
It's the same man who
built the famous road,

23
00:01:03,986 --> 00:01:04,776
the Appian Way.

24
00:01:05,639 --> 00:01:09,169
Over the next five centuries,
Rome added more and more of them.

25
00:01:09,399 --> 00:01:13,349
By the third century AD, eleven
aqueducts carried water into the city.

26
00:01:14,204 --> 00:01:18,854
The longest, the Aqua Marcia, brought water
from springs more than fifty miles away.

27
00:01:19,004 --> 00:01:23,434
And its water was so cold and clear that
Romans considered it the best in the city.

28
00:01:26,316 --> 00:01:27,326
Here's the secret.

29
00:01:27,416 --> 00:01:30,736
An aqueduct is just a channel
that runs very slightly downhill,

30
00:01:30,776 --> 00:01:32,886
all the way from the spring to the city.

31
00:01:33,176 --> 00:01:33,846
No pumps.

32
00:01:34,096 --> 00:01:35,166
Gravity does everything.

33
00:01:36,111 --> 00:01:37,591
The hard part is the slope.

34
00:01:37,631 --> 00:01:40,701
Too steep, and the water rushes
and wears the channel away.

35
00:01:40,871 --> 00:01:42,721
Too flat, and it stops flowing.

36
00:01:43,091 --> 00:01:47,331
So Roman surveyors had to plan a gentle,
steady drop over dozens of miles of hills

37
00:01:47,371 --> 00:01:48,041
and valleys.

38
00:01:49,019 --> 00:01:51,579
Look at the aqueduct that
supplied the city of Nimes,

39
00:01:51,619 --> 00:01:52,849
in southern France.

40
00:01:53,079 --> 00:01:56,809
Over about thirty miles, it drops
only around forty feet in total.

41
00:01:57,651 --> 00:02:00,201
And its most famous
bridge, the Pont du Gard,

42
00:02:00,251 --> 00:02:02,441
is about a hundred and sixty feet high.

43
00:02:02,671 --> 00:02:05,261
Along its whole length,
roughly fifteen hundred feet,

44
00:02:05,371 --> 00:02:08,141
the water channel on top
drops by just one inch.

45
00:02:09,052 --> 00:02:11,902
And the lower arches of the
bridge were built without mortar.

46
00:02:12,232 --> 00:02:14,802
Huge stone blocks, some
weighing several tons,

47
00:02:14,952 --> 00:02:18,122
simply held in place by their
own weight and their perfect cut.

48
00:02:18,998 --> 00:02:23,408
Without lasers or GPS, Roman
surveyors did this with simple tools:

49
00:02:23,598 --> 00:02:26,498
a long wooden table with a water
level, called a chorobates,

50
00:02:26,538 --> 00:02:28,628
and a sighting cross called a groma.

51
00:02:29,603 --> 00:02:31,653
And here's something
most people get wrong.

52
00:02:31,903 --> 00:02:33,793
The famous arches are the exception.

53
00:02:34,203 --> 00:02:37,723
Most of an aqueduct runs underground,
in tunnels and buried channels,

54
00:02:37,763 --> 00:02:39,393
following the shape of the hills.

55
00:02:40,023 --> 00:02:42,593
Arches were built only where
the water had to cross a valley.

56
00:02:43,491 --> 00:02:45,791
Underground, the water
stayed cool and clean,

57
00:02:45,831 --> 00:02:47,361
and it was safe from enemies.

58
00:02:48,091 --> 00:02:51,881
Workers could climb down through shafts
every few hundred feet to clean the channel.

59
00:02:52,776 --> 00:02:55,276
And where a valley was
too deep even for arches,

60
00:02:55,316 --> 00:02:59,656
the Romans used a trick: sealed pipes that
let the water run down one side of the

61
00:02:59,696 --> 00:03:02,566
valley and back up the other,
pushed by its own pressure.

62
00:03:02,956 --> 00:03:05,266
Engineers call it an inverted siphon.

63
00:03:06,061 --> 00:03:08,151
Some aqueducts even powered machines.

64
00:03:08,581 --> 00:03:12,381
Near the town of Arles, in France, water
from an aqueduct ran down a hillside

65
00:03:12,421 --> 00:03:14,891
through sixteen water
wheels, one after another,

66
00:03:14,941 --> 00:03:17,351
turning mills that ground
grain for the region.

67
00:03:17,921 --> 00:03:21,631
Historians sometimes call it one of
the first industrial plants in history.

68
00:03:24,482 --> 00:03:27,502
When the water reached Rome, it
flowed into a distribution tank,

69
00:03:27,542 --> 00:03:30,612
and from there into the city
in a strict order of priority.

70
00:03:31,595 --> 00:03:35,045
First, public fountains, where
anyone could fill a jar for free.

71
00:03:35,295 --> 00:03:36,495
Then, the public baths.

72
00:03:36,535 --> 00:03:40,345
And only last, private homes, for
the rich who paid for their own pipe.

73
00:03:41,226 --> 00:03:42,916
The fountains never had taps.

74
00:03:43,186 --> 00:03:45,286
The water ran all the time, day and night,

75
00:03:45,326 --> 00:03:46,876
and the overflow was useful too.

76
00:03:47,106 --> 00:03:48,836
It flushed the streets and the sewers.

77
00:03:49,521 --> 00:03:52,661
Rome had huge public baths
with hot and cold pools,

78
00:03:52,701 --> 00:03:55,781
and public toilets with
dozens of seats side by side,

79
00:03:55,821 --> 00:03:57,511
where people sat and chatted.

80
00:03:57,761 --> 00:04:00,571
Instead of toilet paper, many
used a sponge on a stick.

81
00:04:01,449 --> 00:04:02,859
The baths were enormous.

82
00:04:03,229 --> 00:04:06,169
The Baths of Caracalla, which
still stand in ruins in Rome,

83
00:04:06,209 --> 00:04:08,689
could take more than a
thousand bathers at a time,

84
00:04:08,729 --> 00:04:10,879
with libraries, gardens and gyms.

85
00:04:11,009 --> 00:04:12,939
All of it filled by aqueduct water.

86
00:04:13,886 --> 00:04:16,236
All that dirty water had to go somewhere.

87
00:04:16,386 --> 00:04:19,136
Under the Roman Forum
runs the Cloaca Maxima,

88
00:04:19,246 --> 00:04:23,316
the great sewer, first built in the
sixth century BC to drain a marsh.

89
00:04:23,846 --> 00:04:26,796
Parts of it still carry rainwater
away from the city today.

90
00:04:29,688 --> 00:04:31,058
Where there's free
water, there are thieves.

91
00:04:31,624 --> 00:04:36,204
In the year ninety-seven AD, a senator
named Frontinus became Rome's water

92
00:04:36,244 --> 00:04:36,684
commissioner.

93
00:04:36,724 --> 00:04:39,484
And he wrote a whole book
about how the system worked,

94
00:04:39,524 --> 00:04:41,174
and about what was going wrong.

95
00:04:42,061 --> 00:04:45,671
He found that a lot of water never
reached the people it was meant for.

96
00:04:45,981 --> 00:04:48,571
Landowners tapped into the
aqueducts in the countryside.

97
00:04:49,041 --> 00:04:52,751
In the city, water workers were
secretly paid to connect private pipes.

98
00:04:53,633 --> 00:04:55,163
Those pipes were made of lead.

99
00:04:55,293 --> 00:04:57,283
The Latin word for lead is plumbum.

100
00:04:57,373 --> 00:04:59,363
And that's where our
word plumbing comes from.

101
00:04:59,961 --> 00:05:04,031
Pipes were stamped with names, so the
city could see who a pipe belonged to.

102
00:05:04,401 --> 00:05:06,641
Frontinus started tearing
out the illegal ones,

103
00:05:06,681 --> 00:05:09,931
and measuring how much water
each aqueduct really delivered.

104
00:05:10,835 --> 00:05:12,865
Frontinus was proud of the system.

105
00:05:13,195 --> 00:05:16,755
He wrote: compare these aqueducts,
with so many indispensable structures,

106
00:05:16,795 --> 00:05:19,945
to the idle pyramids, or
the useless, though famous,

107
00:05:20,075 --> 00:05:20,905
works of the Greeks.

108
00:05:23,906 --> 00:05:25,996
For five hundred years,
the water kept flowing.

109
00:05:26,186 --> 00:05:27,156
And then, it stopped.

110
00:05:27,759 --> 00:05:31,399
In five thirty-seven AD, a
Gothic army besieged Rome,

111
00:05:31,439 --> 00:05:34,189
and cut the aqueducts to
take away the city's water.

112
00:05:34,618 --> 00:05:36,588
And then the Goths tried something clever.

113
00:05:36,818 --> 00:05:39,278
With the water gone, the
aqueduct channels were empty,

114
00:05:39,318 --> 00:05:41,798
and they tried to send
soldiers through one of them,

115
00:05:41,838 --> 00:05:42,948
right into the city.

116
00:05:43,338 --> 00:05:45,268
The Roman commander had expected it.

117
00:05:45,378 --> 00:05:47,408
He had already sealed
the channel with masonry.

118
00:05:48,404 --> 00:05:52,034
After the war, nobody had the money
or the workers to repair them.

119
00:05:52,264 --> 00:05:54,954
The channels filled with
mineral deposits and rubble,

120
00:05:55,044 --> 00:05:57,374
and one by one, the aqueducts went dry.

121
00:05:58,254 --> 00:06:02,084
Rome shrank from a city of hundreds
of thousands into a small town,

122
00:06:02,154 --> 00:06:03,284
huddled along the river.

123
00:06:03,614 --> 00:06:07,384
For centuries, its people went back to
drinking from the Tiber and from wells.

124
00:06:08,328 --> 00:06:10,818
Some aqueducts ended up
as building material.

125
00:06:11,188 --> 00:06:15,628
In Germany, the aqueduct of Cologne had
built up a thick layer of mineral deposit

126
00:06:15,668 --> 00:06:16,938
inside its channel.

127
00:06:17,208 --> 00:06:20,418
In the Middle Ages, people cut
it out, polished it like marble,

128
00:06:20,568 --> 00:06:21,638
and used it in churches.

129
00:06:22,679 --> 00:06:25,729
It took more than a thousand
years to bring the water back.

130
00:06:25,979 --> 00:06:29,809
In the fourteen hundreds, popes
began to restore the old Aqua Virgo.

131
00:06:29,939 --> 00:06:33,799
And in seventeen sixty-two, the Trevi
Fountain was finished at the end of its line,

132
00:06:33,839 --> 00:06:35,969
as a grand monument to that water.

133
00:06:38,869 --> 00:06:41,559
Some Roman aqueducts
didn't need to be rebuilt.

134
00:06:41,809 --> 00:06:46,609
In Segovia, in Spain, an aqueduct bridge
of more than twenty thousand granite blocks

135
00:06:46,649 --> 00:06:50,119
stands without a single drop of
mortar holding the stones together.

136
00:06:50,409 --> 00:06:53,259
It carried water into the city
until the nineteen seventies.

137
00:06:53,514 --> 00:06:55,984
And Roman concrete had
a secret of its own.

138
00:06:56,294 --> 00:07:01,134
In twenty twenty-three, scientists from
MIT found that small white lumps of lime in

139
00:07:01,174 --> 00:07:03,244
the concrete can work like a repair kit.

140
00:07:03,474 --> 00:07:07,144
When a crack lets water in, the
lime dissolves and fills it again.

141
00:07:07,915 --> 00:07:11,145
Two thousand years later, Rome
still gives away its water.

142
00:07:11,355 --> 00:07:13,815
More than two and a half
thousand little street fountains,

143
00:07:13,855 --> 00:07:17,745
called nasoni, run day and night, and
anyone can drink from them for free.

144
00:07:18,661 --> 00:07:21,311
And the coins people throw
into the Trevi Fountain?

145
00:07:21,481 --> 00:07:23,311
They're collected and given to charity.

146
00:07:23,501 --> 00:07:25,371
Well over a million euros a year.

147
00:07:28,125 --> 00:07:29,855
So, how did ancient Rome get its water?

148
00:07:30,359 --> 00:07:32,509
It found clean springs far away.

149
00:07:32,779 --> 00:07:35,639
It built channels that ran
downhill for dozens of miles,

150
00:07:35,679 --> 00:07:38,949
mostly underground, with a slope
so gentle you couldn't see it.

151
00:07:39,139 --> 00:07:42,869
It gave the water away for free, and it
fought the thieves who tried to steal it.

152
00:07:43,689 --> 00:07:45,899
And when the water
stopped, Rome stopped too.

153
00:07:46,089 --> 00:07:48,379
It took the city a thousand
years to get it back.

154
00:07:48,953 --> 00:07:52,093
So, would you drink from a
two-thousand-year-old aqueduct?

155
00:07:52,133 --> 00:07:54,103
And which city should we explain next?

156
00:07:54,193 --> 00:07:55,203
Tell me in the comments.

157
00:07:55,593 --> 00:07:56,743
This was How It's Run.
